{"author":{"id":"8d7a7fea5b07a0d5d081edd516d55afeb51ce9c18b2b2be6ceb30eb17cd9b547"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794380200,"nonce":"2FfmaS/+FJGkLjZRL0ZpGYm59sg=","title":"A text input goes dead after a page rebuild: its signals were owned by the build that created the data","message":"In fs-files (Release build, virtual KWin, typing-test scripts) a text input stays dead in about 10 to 15 percent of runs with fs-blinc-3: nothing is drawn after a key, later keys do not show either, the placeholder stays, a second click into the field does not help. The key reaches the field (its value grows to \"a\", \"ab\", \"abc\"), but the field's revision signal is gone (`revision.try_get() == None`), so the display never follows the value. Before the fix: 3 of 30, 2 of 16 and 5 of 30 runs; with the signals owned by the data: 60 of 60 runs without it (tipptest agent, .scratch/q1b/erste-taste.md).\n\nCause: `TextInputData::new` (text_input/edit.rs), `assemble` (content.rs) and `text_input_focused` (focus.rs) create the revision and focus signals while a build scope is current. The scope owns them and disposes them when the page is rebuilt, but the data lives on: the form keeps its field data for as long as it is open. Same pattern in the text area (text_area/state.rs, content.rs, widget.rs: revision) and in `ScrollRef::watch_y` (selector/scroll_ref.rs: a ref outlives the builds that ask for its offset).\n\nFix: the data owns its signals (`blinc_core::owner::DataSignals`): they are made outside any build scope and disposed when the last clone of the data is dropped, so a replaced field leaves nothing behind. Tests: a field, text area or scroll ref made in a build scope keeps its signals after the scope is disposed, and 1000 such values leave no live signal once dropped.\n\nChecked and not affected: code editor (keyed signals, outside any scope), virtual list (keyed), select/combobox (keyed), the cn components that take their value as a signal from the caller (checkbox, radio, switch, toggle, slider, number input). Per-build signals held only by that build's own closures (date/time pickers, picker popover, calendar, tabs, charts) are not this defect but could lose a signal if an open overlay outlives a rebuild; not checked further.\n\nfs-components side (not changed here): `Slot::new` in fs-components could also create its data under `Owner::detached`.","files":null}]}